{title: Abide with Me}
{artist: Henry Lyte & William Monk}
{key: Eb}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 66}

Verse 1
A[Eb]bide with [Bb]me: fast [Cm]falls the [Ab]even[Eb]tide;
the [Eb]darkness [Ab]deepens — [Eb]Lord, with [Bb]me a[Eb]bide.
When other helpers [Cm]fail and [Ab]comforts [Bb]flee,
[Eb]help of the [Ab]helpless, [Eb]O a[Bb]bide with [Eb]me.

Verse 2
Swift to [Bb]its close ebbs [Cm]out life's [Ab]little
day; [Eb]Earth's [Ab]joys grow [Eb]dim; its [Bb]glories pass
away; Change and [Cm]decay in all [Ab]around [Bb]I
[Eb]see; O Thou [Ab]who [Eb]changest not, abide with [Eb]me.

Verse 3
Not a [Bb]brief glance [Cm]I beg, [Ab]a
passing [Eb]word; [Ab]But as [Eb]Thou [Bb]dwell'st with
Thy disciples, Lord, [Cm]Familiar, condescending, [Ab]patient, [Bb]free.
[Eb]Come not to [Ab]sojourn, [Eb]but abide with [Eb]me.

Verse 4
Come not [Bb]in terrors, as [Cm]the King [Ab]of
kings, [Eb]But [Ab]kind and good, [Eb]with healing in [Bb]Thy wings,
Tears for all [Cm]woes, a heart [Ab]for [Bb]every
[Eb]plea? Come, Friend of [Ab]sinners, [Eb]and thus bide with [Eb]me.

Verse 5
Thou on [Bb]my head in [Cm]early youth [Ab]didst
smile; [Eb]And, [Ab]though rebellious [Eb]and perverse meanwhile, [Bb]Thou hast
not left me, [Cm]oft as I [Ab]left [Bb]Thee,
[Eb]On to the [Ab]close, [Eb]O Lord, abide with [Eb]me.
